/** Editorial translations — siman 228 (176 blocks) — Hatarat nedarim */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '10#_': `The place. Taz wrote Beit Yosef challenged from beginning transgresses do not stand, etc.; some distinguish between sins one is accustomed to — e.g. not lending vessels — most of world fail — therefore not ashamed to tell truth; but uncommon sin — possibly ashamed to say nevertheless would vow; also specifically if others open for him we fear ashamed to answer truth in heart; but if one comes himself saying I ask you release my vow because now clear sin in vowed vow — good opening helps — not brazen to say, etc. — since if wanted could be entirely silent.`,
    '20#א': `His fellow. Maharik wrote likewise if swore before his agent — not release except per sender's intent or agent's intent — agent like person; from need to inform — implies even without suspicion of shame forbidden; Rosh wrote per Yerushalmi ab initio release vow not in his presence only inform release — Gemara here main reason — release only in his presence meaning with his knowledge — Shach; Taz explained per his fellow — fellow confirmed vow and he wants to release; Shach explained for fellow's will.`,
    '20#ב': `And his will. Shach wrote appears Mechaber also holds — must inform and he agrees in will; likewise Taz.`,
    '20#ח': `To punish them. However if did not do him favor — though should not release ab initio — if transgressed and released do not punish — Bach; Beit Yosef wrote however all this for law; but where possible profanation of God's name — forbid — nothing more severe — as Zedekiah punished he and Sanhedrin who released though mitzvah that release.`,
    '20#ל': `To release. Shach wrote implies even if did him favor can release thus; difficult from where — responsum Ramban only no suspicion since died — without favor; but if did favor — reasonable if died cannot release; thus proved, etc.; difficult — see there.`,
    '20#ס': `To remove. As Even HaEzer siman 77; even per those who dispute there — married woman unlike here. Mahari Weil.`,
    '21#א': `Release. Meaning needs sage release and their intent and agreement (but their intent alone does not help as seif 37); Ribash wrote if vowed per community — even if majority agree to nullify vow — not heed until all agree; if vower says now intent was on majority — believed before sages; appears also per siman 211 nowadays all like gentiles regarding need release; but not so stringent to say insufficient release; however appears if specified per so-and-so, etc. — certainly intent all agree — even says intent was majority — not heed — Shach.`,
    '21#ג': `Mitzvah. Then release even without their intent — presumably agree to release — Shach (hire cantor wage is mitzvah Rashdam part 4 siman 214 avoid quarrels mitzvah per many poskim release community swore not take teacher mitzvah Maharival part 3 siman 88 release sage swore not be sage in community mitzvah Raanach part 2 siman 23 release swore not hear Torah from sage mitzvah Mekor Baruch siman 20 marry woman he desires mitzvah Maharam Levi part 4 siman 1 learn Torah with one he desires mitzvah Shiyurei Knesset page 217 appear before officers teach merit of Israel Darchei Moshe mitzvah Maharam part 1 siman 15).`,
    '21#ז': `Presumably. Maharik wrote if those three involved in that vow — all agree per intent of many; all the more if said I vow per your intent — Shach.`,
    '21#ט': `Constraint. Shach wrote means if said anonymously per intent of many; but if specified — no leniency even in constraint and need; Ribash wrote document written per intent of many — law as per intent of many in Gemara — not compelled; should ab initio be stringent not release since some say even anonymous per intent of many no release.`,
    '24#_': `Annuls. Rosh wrote possibly even if husband revokes intent and permits her to vow per others — nothing — since woman anonymously vows per husband's intent; verse tied her annulment to husband — husband can retract permission; Shach wrote though Rosh wrote possibly — nevertheless holds law so; likewise plainly in hints; Tur Even HaEzer siman 96 plainly even if husband gave permission, etc.; not Bach who wrote Rosh uncertain.`,
    '33#ב': `And afterward. All the more if already gave cherem and afterward swore — must tell. Shach; Taz wrote but even without cherem must tell when this one needs testify — behold if not tell, etc.; must be they did not demand testimony — then no transgression as Choshen Mishpat siman 228; but if truly demanded testimony — must tell even without cherem.`,
    '39#ב': `To extend. Written Bedek HaBayit in name of Orach Chayyim — vow partially released — entire vow released; likewise oath — therefore if Reuven owes Shimon maneh by oath by set time and at that time Reuven asks Shimon extend payment until other time — Reuven no longer bound by that oath; Shach wondered what this has to partial release, etc.; must be Orach Chayyim case as if received — therefore no longer bound — entire released; Taz wrote many err in document extending payment by set time — at time lender says I profit you time and does not forgive document — transgresses document; Beit Yosef fix say as if received on condition swear pay by set time and he swears — end quote (see Choshen Mishpat siman 73 seif 6 Semak and Taz there — examine).`,
    '45#ב': `After the fact. Shach wrote Rema follows view holds per intent of many permitted after the fact; but already explained there no release even after the fact — likewise here; this vow in distress like Jacob vowed if God be with me, etc.; but one who vowed in illness not eat cheese harming illness and now regrets — not called vow in distress — can release even ab initio — end quote.`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#א': `Sugya of Rava — Rabbi Nachman: halachah — open with regret, etc. Nedarim page 22 side 2; likewise ruled Rosh there and Rambam chapter 6.`,
    '1#ה': `There — and Rosh in his rulings — from what we say in Bekhorot page 37 side 1: e.g. who said? Rabbi Nachman said e.g. I — and who in these generations is learned and wise like Rabbi Nachman?`,
    '2#א': `Gemara conclusion from incident of Ravina there page 8 side 2.`,
    '3#ג': `Sugya of Abaye there page 76 side 1.`,
    '7#ד': `From incident of one who came before Rabbah bar Rav Huna, etc. there page 21 side 2; Rosh wrote there this opening is not nolad because it is common that relatives and friends of a person are upset over what he vowed.`,
    '10#ב': `Mishnah there page 64 side 2 — and per Abaye's reason there in Gemara: general rule halachah follows Rava — stated only when they dispute regarding law, not where they dispute reason of matter and his reason is reasonable.`,
    '10#ג': `Beit Yosef from Sifrei: if for one mitzvah not so severe for people — as Rosh and Ran wrote.`,
    '11#ב': `Yerushalmi — they wrote it there; Rosh and Ran.`,
    '11#ג': `There in Mishnah and explained there in Gemara side 2: since he was brazen before him to vow them — also at time of regret if no complete regret he will be brazen.`,
    '15#א': `Ribash part 1 siman 36 from mentioned Yerushalmi.`,
    '16#_': `Tur in name of Rambam chapter 6 Laws of Oaths from incident of Ravina who had vow to his wife, etc. Nedarim page 8 side 2 — thus proven in Yerushalmi since they ask chapter 10 of Nedarim what about release via interpreter, etc. and conclude permitted via interpreter because master of matter is also there; likewise Rashba in responsum and incident of Jephthah's daughter proves; likewise Ribash in name of Raah and Hagahot Maimoniyot on Rambam and Hagahot Mordekhai on Kiddushin.`,
    '18#_': `Responsum Ramban siman 201 and responsum Rashba.`,
    '20#א': `Baraita Nedarim page 65 side 1 and in Mishnah commentary I wrote with law conclusion and some other laws attached that I compiled here in exile and called it Kli Golah — may God merit me to complete it. I proved one word was omitted in copy of this law — should read: for that one who vowed per his intent and knowledge — meaning and will, etc. since he vowed for him for favor he received from him; as Rambam wrote in Laws of Oaths chapter 7 and Beit Yosef in this siman per Rambam and per Rosh and in name of Raam and Raavad; likewise Tosafot there in name of Rabbeinu Tam, Rashba, Mordekhai, Tur, Ran, Semag, Maharik in responsum and many poskim except Ramban; and for one answer in Tosafot Gittin as cited below; likewise proven from Mechaber's words who wrote but if of himself, etc. without his knowledge — implies need not his intent and will but must inform him as Yerushalmi says due to suspicion as Rambam and other poskim wrote; I now saw in composition of Rav Taz who wrote what Shulchan Aruch says unless he informed him, etc. means his will in this — in my view Mechaber did not come to be terse but to explain; even though Rema wrote both laws in name of some say — nevertheless matter is necessary as I wrote in my view and as Rav Taz also wrote.`,
    '20#ד': `Raavad and Rashba in responsum and Maharik siman 52.`,
    '20#ה': `Ran in responsum from words of Tosafot Gittin page 35 side 2; likewise Rashba in responsum.`,
    '20#ו': `There in Tosafot for latter answer; likewise in responsa to Rambam simanim 248, 249, Ran; per Rashba who said in Yerushalmi due to suspicion.`,
    '23#_': `There from Tosafot, Rosh, Ran, Mordekhai on sugya of Amemar — I cited above beginning seif 21; Semag in name of Geonim; Rashba in responsum; likewise appears from Rambam's words.`,
    '25#ד': `Ran in responsum; likewise Rashba in responsum.`,
    '25#ה': `There in Rashba's responsum in name of Raah.`,
    '26#_': `Rashba responsum — concludes there community release cuts for her like husband's annulment as below siman 234; Maharik wrote in root 51 (and below 229 seif 2).`,
    '31#_': `Rosh general 5 siman 4 and general 7 siman 5; Ribash part 1 siman 73.`,
    '32#_': `Ran in responsum; likewise Rashba in responsum.`,
    '33#_': `Semag siman 238 in name of ruling of Gaon and responsum of Geonim; likewise seen in responsa Rabbi Yosef Tuv Elem and Mordekhai in name of responsum Rashi and responsum Ramban siman 280 and many.`,
    '35#_': `Rosh in responsum general 5 siman 4 from Midrash Yelamdenu — thus was oath of Joshua and oath of concubine in Gibeah and oath of Saul — decree and excommunication.`,
    '41#א': `Rashba responsum (and Ribash per Rosh and Ramban siman 259 and end of Sefer Kol Bo).`,
    '41#ב': `Per Ribash part 1 siman 99 and part 2 siman 158; Rav Beit Yosef brought them in this siman.`,
    '42#א': `Rashba in responsum; likewise written in responsa to Ramban siman 275.`,
    '48#_': `Rashba responsum (and duplicated Choshen Mishpat siman 73 seif 9): because in vows we follow people's language and it is biblical doubt — we rule stringently.`,
    '50#_': `Ribash part 2 siman 145.`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '5#_': `(there s.k. 34) But we do not rule, etc. — see Shach s.k. 56 that here all agree law is thus.`,
    '10#_': `(there in Shach small seif 111) In my humble view without, etc. — however one could say not comparable to siman 220 seif 4 and what Rosh wrote chapter Konam — because in language of baraita also first day of Rosh Chodesh is included in Rosh Chodesh for future; nevertheless one could say when he says on Rosh Chodesh his intent is second day of Rosh Chodesh since second is like extended day as both are Rosh Chodesh.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#ב': `That learned, etc. and he is, etc. — thus Rosh chapter 3 siman 3 and Ran Nedarim 8:2 s.v. excommunication, etc. 23:1 s.v. and release, etc. 8:1 s.v. said — not like Ramban who requires sage specifically. Rambam's view: laypeople need not be wise, etc. and Abaye.`,
    '1#ג': `And also know, etc. Rosh there and Ran 8:1 in name of Yerushalmi.`,
    '3#א': `Three times. Tur and Rambam imply once suffices. Beit Yosef. In his commentary chapter 10 wrote three times; but Rosh at end of Yoma as Tur wrote however it appears, etc. therefore, etc. (collection) three times — as written end chapter 4 deaths vow released, etc. even though there only once — three times certainly does not add or detract as written chapter 3 parah cedar, etc. hyssop, etc. likewise chapter 1 Niddah (11:1) she squeezed and saw, etc. though Tosafot emend there should read three times not necessary (likewise Tosafot there 39:2 s.v. therefore) as written above and in many places (end). (collection) three times — as end chapter 4 deaths vow released [likewise chapter the ship (74:1) text in printed editions annulled for you annulled for you] even though only wrote once it is three times as chapter 3 parah hyssop this, etc. and Menachot chapter Rabbi Yishmael (65:1) and other places — but once we never find (end).`,
    '3#ב': `Or, etc. see above siman 234 seif 37.`,
    '5#_': `And if asked, etc. Rambam — because he benefited release is not void as Kesef Mishneh and this is what Gemara Bava Batra chapter 5 (74:1) said annulled for you and on Choshen Mishpat there.`,
    '6#_': `Swore, etc. as above previous seif and Beit HaGadol and Abaye in Yerushalmi commentary cited.`,
    '7#ב': `However, etc. — we require uprooting vow from root as chapter HaMedir (74:2) what difference sage, etc. and this is meaning Rashbag himself troubled to find opening for Rav Sachora; likewise from what is written your heart upon you as Rosh there.`,
    '7#ג': `And some say return, etc. and there is one who, etc. see Mordekhai chapter 3 Shevuot who expanded — wrote Rambam: chapter 3 Nedarim we conclude halachah open with regret and implies entire sugya there with mere regret as your heart upon you, etc. not as Rashi chapter HaDar who requires finding opening, etc. likewise there wrote one who opens leniency says whereas, etc. and Tosafot Eruvin 64:2 s.v. open, etc. wrote there Rabbi Yosef Bekhor Shor wrote three regrets: one nolad and do not open; two was in world but did not know — all open; three thing not yet in world but regularly comes — on this they dispute if open this regret; from your heart upon you after finding opening told him still you stand as at vow time — teaching caution not to lie; deduced from Gemara from your heart upon you implies matter occurred after vow, etc. (end).`,
    '8#_': `Cherem, etc. Beit HaGadol and Beit Yosef's question what connection one who excommunicates another, etc. — I do not know if cherem is vow — does one who excommunicates another from his property need release? Even though siman 17 divides writing likewise, etc. not relevant here; also proof from what is written 7:2 sage excommunicates himself and annuls himself — implies need not sage release; also they said annuls not releases — implies like husband's annulment without opening and regret; likewise from what is written as that which 47, etc. there certainly no opening and regret [see above siman 215].`,
    '10#א': `Vow, etc. or, etc. Rosh there s.v. that you were, etc. and s.v. on, etc.`,
    '10#ב': `That vow is as if, etc. Ran s.v. Rabbi Tzadok, etc. and s.v. they said, etc. however concede, etc.`,
    '10#ג': `Or he says, etc. Rosh s.v. they open, etc. and say, etc. and s.v. they said, etc. and no, etc.`,
    '10#ד': `That person is not, etc. and siman 11 from Taz that he is not, etc. — cited Abaye's reason since sugya there 22:1 as his words; even though opening, etc. all the more in such case as written; and for this reason, etc.`,
    '15#ג': `And if there is, etc. Ran wrote 1 s.v. and regarding. Tosafot, etc.`,
    '16#_': `Must, etc. and they release, etc. Ran there s.v. master what, etc. [likewise Ribash wrote and incident of Jephthah's daughter in Bereishit Rabbah chapter 60 proves].`,
    '17#ב': `And even, etc. — not comparable to what is written in Taz and if there is, etc. — there permitted by law only due to vow; also reason because it is transgression and if there is stumbling block — on contrary mitzvah to release — not so here; by law not permitted and if released not permitted (end responsum).`,
    '17#ה': `And likewise. — also annulment as written 7:2 and annuls himself as above.`,
    '18#_': `Who, etc. doubts in responsum whether vow takes effect when Passover arrives or Shavuot — ruled stringently not to release until Shavuot; even if transgresses vow on Shavuot nothing since they release him uproots vow from root as written chapter 9:2 and he removed, etc. and Ran there s.v. and he removed, etc. But Rosh there s.v. and he shaved wrote when he sheared, etc. and this is Shulchan Aruch; I think good, etc.`,
    '20#ד': `For the oath. — otherwise many favored Jacob and likewise Pharaoh to Joseph.`,
    '20#ו': `And oath was, etc. — in such case shame applies as Ran there wrote some say, etc. due to shame meaning, etc.; thus answers Tosafot Gittin question no benefit to orphans in forbidding her eating; likewise Ran in Nedarim there.`,
    '20#ז': `And some say need, etc. — per reason of suspicion — meaning for both reasons to be stringent as Ran there; likewise Ribash ruled stringently for both reasons.`,
    '20#ל': `And if swore, etc. Ran in Nedarim there s.v. the cited and that of Gittin also not, etc.`,
    '20#נ': `And if died, etc. as above no suspicion; Shach's words s.k. 53 are error and mistake — per this view no distinction between did favor forever need not his intent and agreement; Shach brought proof from holders of first view and followed what Rema wrote in some say s.k. 52 same mistake from Ribash who is Rema's some say who ruled both reasons stringently; Rema already wrote there some say need, etc. and if already died, etc. — all refers when vowed of himself, etc. unlike here per latter view.`,
    '21#ב': `And some say, etc. per Rashba's explanation and Semag and many — because their regrets unequal since each regrets for different reason unlike if mitzvah in release all agree one mind; therefore husband annuls in all as siman 234 need not opening and regret.`,
    '21#ג': `One who swore, etc. as written Berakhot 6:1 prayer not heard except in synagogue; we say one who has synagogue in his city, etc. why does master not come to synagogue, etc. (there 7:2, 8:1) entire sugya there; not per Rabbeinu Tam who wrote for mitzvah matter not release except if those many involved in it; he brought proof from what is written 46:1 Rabbi Yehoshua why Rabbi Yosi, etc. even though mitzvah to kill them — they challenged him there no mitzvah as Sotah 35:2 for Meret, etc. and Rashi and Tosafot there s.v. to include, etc. did not write thus; also verse does not imply — wrote why did you deceive us, etc. and now, etc. [but Tosafot Gittin 35:2 s.v. but distinguished between per many and vowing publicly wrote further per many, etc. you know, etc.] they brought proof not per Rabbeinu Tam from sugya Makkot 16:2 but coercion no — where, etc.`,
    '21#ה': `Or due to needs of, etc. as written chapter 23 Shabbat 150:1 and chapter 1 Ketubot; see Even HaEzer siman 70 seif 4 and siman 93 seif 4.`,
    '21#ז': `And specifically if, etc. — otherwise every vow mitzvah to release as Nedarim 59:1 whereas, etc. — except reason there lest cannot fulfill as Rambam end Laws of Vows; if Elijah comes says can fulfill — no mitzvah to release; likewise here; his words strained — more plausible latter answer Maharik wrote there specifically great mitzvah like that Gemara case and similar.`,
    '21#ח': `And if those, etc. — reason presumably agree as above unlike here.`,
    '21#י': `And such as, etc. Tosafot 36:1 s.v. but, etc. and Rosh there but many dispute; see Shach.`,
    '21#כ': `And do not rely, etc. — likewise implied from Rosh's answer in Gittin and Nedarim to Tosafot Gittin 35:2 s.v. and lest fear. You asked between, etc. he answered because after the fact their release is release as above seif 21 — implies here even after the fact nothing.`,
    '21#מ': `One who swore, etc. — likewise reason due to their regret, etc.`,
    '22#ד': `Or per, etc. — not per Tosafot Gittin there s.v. three appear, etc. you asked per, etc. but in Shevuot 29:2 s.v. so that wrote do not learn, etc. and Rosh there and Ran there wrote some say even per God specifically in Torah and mitzvot God does not agree to release unlike discretionary matter — and this is Hagahah however if, etc. and some are lenient, etc.`,
    '25#ה': `Even if they mentioned, etc. — their intent nevertheless only to be stringent — who will transgress their decree; as written cherem in your midst Israel and written Joshua swore, etc.; see Shevuot 36:1 and this is Tosafot Rabbah Daat because oath is cherem meaning in such case; reason Ran, Rashba and many wrote since they practiced thus likewise as if they stipulated to be forbidden until they agree to release — like woman's vow we say (Shabbat 46:2 and Shevuot) per husband she vows; therefore specifically what they practiced before vowing and excommunicating per custom; unlike if they add stringency new matter — and this is if they stipulated, etc. and likewise, etc.`,
    '25#ו': `And some say, etc. — likewise responsum Rosh — thus disputes first view — per first view need sage inquiry unlike other cheremot as Shulchan Aruch not like Shach; per his view even per many they release themselves without sage release; his view all they added all was intent like cherem but majority poskim dispute as Shulchan Aruch and Beit Yosef; what Shulchan Aruch wrote siman 229 seif 4 likewise community, etc. must release — per this view via sage release.`,
    '26#_': `There is not, etc. as above cherem is annulment; likewise written seif 8 and in annulment we say chapter 4 Nazir cuts for her; Tosafot there 41:2 s.v. husband, etc. but husband, etc.`,
    '27#ג': `And can release, etc. see above siman 234 seif 36 — per Shulchan Aruch there and Ribash siman 285. (collection) and can, etc. likewise Hagahot Alfasi chapter 2 Bava Batra siman 12 s.v. and in this. Raavyah disagrees, etc. likewise Choshen Mishpat end siman 256 Hagahah if they released one, etc. (end).`,
    '30#ג': `And releasers can, etc. — must be we do not ask in vows 73:1 master what, etc. except because written her, etc. and Ribash siman 461.`,
    '31#א': `Community, etc. as written chapter 1 Horayot 3:2. Rashba.`,
    '33#ה': `And forbidden to transgress, etc. as written chapter 25 (130:1).`,
    '35#א': `Impossible, etc. — even those already in world cannot be sworn unless agrees and answers amen as chapter 3 Shevuot 29:2 — Ribash siman 285 wrote Ramban, etc. one who did not answer amen after oath oath does not take effect at all — need not release — we never find sworn by another's mouth who did not answer amen liable except testimony oath by Torah decree; Raavad stringent on oath cantor swears even if did not answer amen because swears per their intent and will (end) unlike here; and this is in Pirkei DeRabbi Eliezer great oath was and Israel swore — only to inform you cherem is oath, etc. likewise Saul's oath excommunication and cherem and Joshua's oath; therefore even on those born applies as Kings (1 16) and this is but excommunication, etc. as Joshua (7:13) cherem in your midst, etc. and this is siman 215 Nedarim, etc. not, etc. even if they mentioned, etc.`,
    '35#ג': `And some are stringent. — Darkei Moshe per Rosh general 7 siman 4 they enacted against first cherem and per enactment documents made and said documents not disqualified — unlike chapter Temurah (4:2) per Rava if he did not help — there biblical mitzvot cannot nullify unlike here; also sage uproots vow from root and when they release cherem as if never was as Shevuot 27:28; likewise nazir who became impure and drank wine then asked on naziritehood not lashed; Darkei Moshe wrote this contradicts what he wrote seif 6 first view; but Maggid Mishneh wrote here after cherem release enactment applies retroactively; likewise Shach. Nevertheless doubt what he wrote sage uproots, etc. — this not in community cherem as above seif 26; likewise Maggid Mishneh challenged; what Shach answered in Hagahah here discusses after sage release — language of responsum does not imply; likewise what he wrote not like that of Temurah, etc. on its own meaning in Hagahah did not write thus and Choshen Mishpat siman 208 what is written and see there.`,
    '36#_': `One who vowed, etc. like consecration vows — can say release as Arakhin 23:1 and Rambam chapter 4 Laws of Vows; likewise charity vows as Semak.`,
    '37#א': `One who swore per, etc. Ribash siman 285 briefly: I was asked, etc. I answered one who swore per can his fellow release him forever not via sage like husband — we say all who vow, etc. and layperson is no lesser than husband with unspecified husband; this ancient view Ramban brought but incorrect — if so suffices by law like case if heard remained silent vow upheld though with husband on day he heard Torah decree for household peace not applicable to other — know we say there 72:1 divorced and returned, etc. even though per husband was — Ramban's view; however even here can argue even at time of hearing cannot nullify — what is written per husband she vows means not in her heart this vow stand if husband objects; but one who says per so-and-so perhaps intent to evade artifice as written 25:1 when they make him swear, etc.; also do not learn from husband's annulment — else should say any language revealing husband's will void; we teach one who says, etc. and husband who said, etc. — certainly Torah decree since even standing screaming vows per intent husband cannot annul — Tosafot; likewise husband annuls even if vowed per many [Ran 73:2 s.v. and some derive, etc.] — Rosh and Tur not so as siman 231 in Shulchan Aruch Hagahah but if, etc. wrote in responsum reason all who vow, etc. is primary reason since relied in many places chapter 3 Yevamot (29:2) chapter 3 Shabbat chapter 10 Nedarim and Niddah 46:2; Tosafot there s.v. as Rav, etc. or because, etc.`,
    '37#ב': `Unless, etc. as chapter 2 (18:2) unspecified vows to be stringent and their explanation to be lenient; see above siman 208 seif 1.`,
    '40#_': `Father, etc. — we rule like Rabbi Yonatan Rava holds like him chapter HaShoel. Rosh chapter 5 Chullin 62 and above siman 16 seif 2.`,
    '41#_': `One who swore, etc. responsum Ramban and Rashba: one who swore, etc. every day, etc. — two matters: accepted to do something without tying to time; accepted to do by set time — not dependent on each other; but if swore, etc. like swore on loaf to eat today and did not eat — does not help to eat later need not eat later as beginning Shevuot 3:2 — learn; this distinction like Yerushalmi Rosh brought chapter 1 Shabbat and chapter 1 Taanit between vow and fast unspecified day versus this day (end); Maharam Padua siman 72 wrote distinction between first and second section — second said his vow and fixed time at once; Rosh general 8 siman 6 wrote even if not demanded within time oath stands because time is spur and essence oath on payment and document language — one would say swore to eat this loaf today, etc. there not obligated to eat that loaf essence oath on time; here obligated to pay essence on payment time to spur — see; Maharam Padua Rosh does not dispute since there document language was thus. Ribash wrote once time passed exempt like swore on loaf, etc. likewise Rashba; even though Rosh wrote opposite his words not plausible; even per his words can be lenient here payment of debt differs; in another responsum wrote to distinguish as Hagahah here whereas not in his case; therefore Rav also distinguished between first and second section — his words astonishing here certainly cannot distinguish except per Maharam Padua or per Maggid Mishneh distinguish said with lamed or with bet — language proves; also wrote some say, etc. which is Rosh's view; likewise Taz challenged.`,
    '43#_': `And some say specifically, etc. Gemara Shevuot there said due to community cherem; said if came while still, etc. — certainly not community intent; since released one hour released forever and vow partially released entirely released; but if came afterward like chapter 9 konam this house, etc. — nolad; there 65:1 one who vows from friend, etc. likewise seif 20 man and woman, etc.; Darkei Moshe wrote possibly Rashba of first view only disputes to exempt from penalty but on oath needs regret and release — do not open nolad; Hagahah but if, etc. I think even if came while still in wrath needs regret and release for above reason do not open nolad; specifically community cherem said thus as above.`,
    '45#ד': `As law, etc. as above reason many agree to mitzvah matter all the more before God.`,
    '45#ו': `Rather, etc. as written in Hagahah all the more here as before God.`,
    '46#א': `(collection) they release, etc. from what is written 72:2 way of sage, etc. told him all vows, etc. and likewise, etc. and Ran there 73 in Tosefta of Mekhilta how, etc. even disputant in annulment in second section because written her (end).`,
    '46#ב': `And for how many, etc. from chapter 10 (73:1) master what annuls for two, etc. — specifically because written her, etc.`,
    '46#ג': `But one who, etc. as 90:1 konam, etc. asked, etc. konam, etc. asked, etc. and Beit HaGadol; specifically in such case second not take effect until first released as Shach.`,
    '47#ב': `(collection) and if need, etc. — vows of coercion permitted; even in such case called vows of coercion as Nazir 11:2 and other answer in vows of coercion, etc.; see responsum Ramban siman 273 and likewise siman 232 seif 12 in Hagahah and if commanded, etc. (end).`,
    '50#ב': `And if they stipulated, etc. as written (20:1) vow in cherem and said in his cherem, etc. as Ran there because in second section their meaning is to be stringent not lenient unlike in first section all the more cannot explain against meaning of enactment.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `No expert individual. Implies if there is expert individual three laypeople do not help; however not necessary — poskim wrote and if there is no expert because certainly burdensome with three more than one therefore said if no expert need three specifically; nevertheless three suffice even where expert individual exists. However in Gemara Bekhorot 36:2 regarding permitting firstborn explicitly three synagogue members cannot release where expert exists; see Raavad's commentary on Mishnah there who wrote explicitly also for vows three do not help where expert individual exists. Therefore though poskim wrote no expert nowadays — still proper to be stringent where sage or av bet din exists not to release with three laypeople only but join two with rabbi; especially concern Rambam chapter 6 Laws of Oaths law 5 every established sage in generation is expert individual for vows. I found in responsum Hatam Sofer siman 129 who expanded to prove even where expert exists three laypeople can release; he brought Ran Nedarim 23 who wrote explicitly ab initio cannot due to honor of sage but after the fact his vow is permitted (end).`,
    '2#_': `And relatives. See responsum Rabbi Akiva Eiger in omissions to siman 73 who wrote appears specifically relatives — disqualification not in essence since fit to judge others; likewise at night — another matter causes it i.e. time; not so women; likewise minors unfit for vow release; even per Tur Choshen Mishpat siman 6 son of thirteen who did not bring two hairs fit to judge — means unknown if brought hairs — only for money but truly for chalitzah judges need verification brought hairs — likewise vow release we do not rely on Rava's presumption and need verification therefore careful to take releasers only men whose beards filled. He also wrote in responsum one unwilling to specify vow because vowed in business contradiction and unwilling reveal secret to others — has one man willing reveal secret to him but young beard not yet filled. Advice: take four releasers — three whose beards filled and fourth this one who knows his secret; before him alone specify vow — suffices because biblically need not specify vow; for rabbinic need to specify again rely on Rava's presumption since reached years of maturity brought signs and this fourth also among releasers — vow specified to one releaser (end).`,
    '11#_': `And oath was for Yitro's benefit. Shach s.k. 43 disputes him — his view: with favor even if oath not for friend's benefit there is no view permitting. See responsum Zera Avraham part 4 siman 9 on Reuven servant of Shimon expelled from house because all profit lost among sinful men; when saw evil ended pleaded Shimon reconcile and Shimon agreed on condition swear per his intent not wear silk garments ten years; asked rabbi if release via sage not per master's intent since no benefit reaches master — he ruled can release not in his presence (end). Astonishing he did not cite Shach here that even if oath not for friend's benefit all who received favor cannot release without his intent. I found in Shaar HaMelekh chapter 6 Laws of Oaths law 7 they wondered on this; he raised conversely Shach's words astonishing and proved from Rashba and Ran case of woman vowing for orphans opposite; also in name of Mahariyat part 1 siman 1 on Reuven obligated money to Shimon by set time and forbade meat and wine if not pay by that time — Rav wrote he has remedy to ask on vow though taught one forbidden benefit from friend, etc. all the more where received favor as loan in distress, etc. case unlike Moses and Zedekiah because here no benefit to friend in vow's standing, etc. (end). Clear from his words all who no benefit to friend in oath though received favor — all permit without his intent opposite Shach (end). [See responsum Hatam Sofer s.s. Yoreh Deah].`,
    '13#_': `For mitzvah need. Beit HaTovim [see responsum Hatam Sofer siman 226 explained where struck fist which is more severe possibly all agree even for mitzvah do not release — thus resolves Taz's question there further]. See responsum Noda BiYehudah second part Choshen Mishpat siman 117 who wrote even per disputants who do not release even for mitzvah matter — specifically when harm to friend in this release like Moses going to Egypt though mitzvah there loss to Yitro since he was shepherd; but otherwise all agree release. Nevertheless regarding main law not release without friend's intent — Shach's view s.k. 43 that even if oath not for friend's benefit nevertheless for mitzvah matter all agree if no benefit to friend in this they release (end).`,
    '19#_': `Said unspecified. Beit HaTovim in name of Shach; see responsum Shaar Efraim siman 72 page 53 side 2 and Tashbetz part 1 siman 155 and responsum Shemesh Tzedakah Yoreh Deah beginning siman 23.`,
    '20#_': `In time of constraint. Shach challenged Rabbi Avigdor and therefore wrote do not be lenient at all even in constraint and need; see responsum Yeri'ot HaOhel siman 39 who expanded to answer Rabbi Avigdor well (end); also Shaar HaMelekh chapter 6 Laws of Oaths law 7 wrote to reconcile Rabbi Avigdor (end).`,
    '29#_': `To swear those born. See responsum Noda BiYehudah second part Choshen Mishpat siman 146 who expanded to prove even for those in world impossible oath applies to one not present with them even if majority of assembly and prophets and Urim and Tumim there (end).`,
    '31#_': `And some are stringent. Shach s.k. 96; see responsum Noda BiYehudah second part Even HaEzer siman 129 section 3 what he challenged and his words are in responsum Rosh. [See responsum Hatam Sofer s.s. 6 on this].`,
    '32#_': `Who vowed to ascend to Land of Israel. See responsum Rashbash siman 2 who wrote Rosh's reason because ascent itself only preparatory to mitzvah — mitzvah is dwelling in land as written you shall inherit and dwell as Ramban wrote; Rashbatz disagreed — if so one who swore to ascend to Land can ask on vow and not like swore to fulfill mitzvah cannot ask; but if swore to dwell in Land and wants to ask — do not release (end). Beit HaTovim of RiT siman 243 s.k. 2 above.`,
    '35#_': `To extend. Beit HaTovim; what he cited in name of Taz many err, etc. see below siman 232 s.k. 15 from this. What Taz wrote in his language and does not forgive document, etc. — implies if forgives document or oath helps; see responsum Noda BiYehudah part 4 siman 67 who proved even if forgives document or oath does not help unless forgives debt — then fist-strike or oath forgiven automatically (end).`,
    '37#_': `For set time. See Sefer Tiferet Lemoshe who wrote even if says on day so-and-so transgresses every day since obligates himself against friend — like swore per friend, etc. (end).`,
    '39#_': `His deeds. See responsum Noda BiYehudah second part Choshen Mishpat siman 204 who wrote means apostatized or became promiscuous in sexual matters or other transgressions and ugly deeds heard about him; but for one time not yet fallen name corrupted (end); also responsum Darchei Noam Yoreh Deah siman 244 and responsum Shemesh Tzedakah part 4 siman 43.`,
    '40#_': `Sister of betrothed. Beit HaTovim; see on this responsum Beit Hillel siman 8 and responsum Har HaCarmel Even HaEzer siman 3. See responsum Noda BiYehudah Yoreh Deah siman 69 who wrote Rosh permitted cancel engagement due to sister of betrothed — a fortiori father's father — sages said impurity until three generations did not cease and written to fourth generation; also said ben Puti, etc. (end).`,
    '42#_': `In time of distress. See responsum Noda BiYehudah second part Choshen Mishpat siman 199 on woman difficult in childbirth vowed her garment to synagogue for mantle (see responsum Hatam Sofer siman 161 if permitted woman's garment for synagogue holy garment) [see below siman 282 Taz in name of Maharil brought also Beit HaTovim there] — husband can annul since even if vowed per many he can annul; only doubt if need annul at all and if vow takes effect at all since garment from father's house nevertheless not less than melog property — in any case can annul — certainly if no garment would need make another; since can annul need no opening or regret — see there.`,
    '43#_': `No release. See responsum Rabbi Levi ben Chaviv siman 3 who wrote appears distinction between one who vowed in his distress and explained if God saves me I vow thus — no release; but vowed unspecified without condition whether God saves or not — can release (end); see Shach s.k. 105 who divides thus and cites responsum Maharam Mintz on one who vowed in distress to go to Land if God saves, etc. — must refer also when explained if God saves go to Land.`,
    '47#_': `On Rosh Chodesh Adar. See Beit HaTovim in name of Taz; see responsum BaShamayim Rosh siman 14 and Hagahot Kessef HaRosena there on this.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '2#_': `(there Shach s.k. 9) And now custom releasers. Maharikash wrote questioner must stand and after the fact even sitting Radbaz siman 326.`,
    '4#_': `(there seif 7) And even opening from body of vow; likewise one who forbade himself conditionally if transgresses certain sin and comes and says I transgressed and regrets — they tell him had you known you transgressed your vow would you have vowed; even though some distinguish between tying to others' intent versus own intent — also in his case his impulse overpowered therefore also called nolad present. Beit Yosef in name of Ramban.`,
    '5#_': `(there) Opening and also regret — in Taz Rabbi Betzalel Ashkenazi wrote for consecration vows all agree need opening and regret.`,
    '9#_': `(there Hagahah) And one who vowed not to laugh at essence of law — is as if swore per many.`,
    '11#_': `(there seif 17) Until Iyar enters; if can release before entry of Iyar — one could say depends if biblical doubt from Torah stringently vow takes effect since biblically forbidden eat on festival; but per those holding biblical doubt from Torah leniently — vow not called taking effect — learn from Ran responsum siman 50 (end); I doubt if release before 24th before vow takes effect; see below siman 234 seifim 31-33; apparently proof from Ran responsum siman 27 — see there he asked how find release behold every time not betrothed vow not take effect — must release moment before she receives kiddushin money; nevertheless can reject since vowed all money given her as kiddushin — all given as kiddushin though kiddushin not take effect forbidden in benefit — then release vow when given as kiddushin vow takes effect since given as kiddushin and after release truly betrothed with it — doubt in law.`,
    '18#_': `(there Shach s.k. 75) Need not release before them. In my humble view not proof — one could say ab initio needs before them or with knowledge only after the fact permitted; see Shach s.k. 69.`,
    '20#_': `(there) And is only like decree. See responsum Mahariyat part 1 siman 67 who expanded and made dispute between Ramban and Rosh on core reason community cherem need not release; likewise expanded in responsum Maharival part 2 siman 72; see responsum Ram Alsheikh siman 75 — no oath applies to cherem; responsum Tamim De'im siman 213; Rashdam Yoreh Deah siman 27; Mishpetei Shmuel siman 69; Mahari Adriabi siman 9; but view Ram Metrani part 1 siman 695 oath applies to cherem.`,
    '26#_': `(there seif 36) To ascend to Land. See Mahariyat part 2 Yoreh Deah siman 28; responsum Penei Moshe part 1 siman 5; responsum Torat Chesed Rav Moshe Chasdai printed at beginning of book end of indexes.`,
    '27#_': `(there seif 37) See below siman 231 raises presumption of friend where agreement in cherem and his deeds did not help — see Kenesset HaGedolah Choshen Mishpat siman 200 in Hagahot Tur section 250.`,
    '30#_': `(there seif 39) Not in his power — see Even HaEzer siman 38 in Beit Shmuel s.k. 134.`,
    '31#_': `(there Shach s.k. 101) And in Orach Chayyim wrote. Likewise responsum Geonim siman 326; there siman 299 — wrote like Rashba not in his power to extend — implies like Shach.`,
    '33#_': `(there Taz s.k. 49) I wonder since has two meanings — see responsum Chakham Tzvi siman 4; responsum Maharival siman 63 siman 68; responsum Mahariyat part 1 siman 27 and siman 127 end; responsum Ba'ei Chayei to Kenesset HaGedolah Choshen Mishpat section 1 s.k. 70; responsum Lechem Rav siman 191; responsum Shaar Efraim siman 133; Maharival part 3 siman 17; writings of Maharai siman 73; responsum Pri HaAretz Ram Mizrachi Yoreh Deah siman 9; Even HaEzer siman 65.`,
    '34#_': `(there seif 41) To do matter on day so-and-so. See responsum Raanach part 2 siman 67; responsum Turei Chayyim Maharchash part 1 siman 50 and part 3 siman 9.`,
    '35#_': `(there Shach s.k. 102) One matter is — see Beit Yosef above s.k. 60; doubt — later found in Azahayi who challenged thus in Maggid Mishneh chapter 4 law 2 s.v. know interest, etc. and left in doubt.`,
    '38#_': `(there seif 43 Hagahah) And so to me main — see responsum Rashdam Choshen Mishpat siman 7.`,
    '39#_': `(there seif 45) No release. See responsum Yachin uVoaz siman 58 of Taz who wrote reason regarding World to Come is now before vow takes effect release does not help; see responsum Mahari Levi siman 18 and 20; responsum Shnei HaMeorot HaGedolim siman 14.`,
    '40#_': `(there Hagahah) Maharam Padua siman 10 should be siman 70; see responsum Maharal ben Chaviv siman 3 there is room to say and distinguish specifically one who says if God saves me, etc. but unspecified vow — can say has release; see responsum Avodat HaGoren siman 10.`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#ב': `And they are learned and wise — meaning they study laws with them and understand; implies even if none among them learned alone but all learned from others and are wise — they release. Some explain per Choshen Mishpat siman 3 regarding judges — if none learned and knows rationales in laws disqualified to judge — vow release is not judgment as will be explained; this is not like Perishah in this siman; Taz wrote and they learned and are somewhat wise so they know what they release — implies need not be very wise — only somewhat wise enough to know what they release — and so practiced.`,
    '3#א': `Three times — not literally — once also suffices; done thus to strengthen matter; Taz; implies after the fact once suffices; likewise Beit Yosef and Bach in name of Rambam need not say only once.`,
    '4#_': `Since must be seated — but forbidden person need not be seated at all; certainly need stand since not judgment; now custom releasers sit and forbidden stands — proof from Yerushalmi chapter Naarah HaMe'orasah: vows asked only wrapped and seated — questioner seated and asker must stand, etc. (end); though in our Gemara and poskim releasers stand — that is for regret but for opening need seated; Yerushalmi discusses opening; now custom release all via opening as below seif 7 Hagahah; in Gemara Eruvin chapter HaDar (64:2) incident of Rabban Gamliel — forbidden to hire to teach as siman 242 seif 13 likewise forbidden to release vows; likewise Keritot (13:2) regarding Rav Acha his vow, etc. — however one could say specifically when releasing via opening needing investigation and resolution of mind — as Gemara concludes incident of Rabban Gamliel was thus.`,
    '6#_': `Swore not to be tax collector or trustee of city — I think he benefits from city authority which gentiles and converts are not appointed over; also public benefits since their work done through him; since he benefits cannot ask sage of city until here Rashba's words in Beit Yosef. Darkei Moshe wrote even though only vowed this not to be collector or trustee alone and did not forbid city benefit — nevertheless cannot ask sage of city since forbade collectorship and trusteeship bringing him benefit from city; his intent on benefits of collectorship and trusteeship; since via sage release this benefit comes — transgressed vow; thus seems to him main (end). Per this Rashba follows Rambam's view as Beit Yosef wrote; Taz wrote in his explanation not like Perishah — examine; nevertheless per poskim who forbid due to conflict of interest as I wrote s.k. 9 here without favor cannot ask — clear.`,
    '14#_': `And reason for which, etc. (wonder) — I never saw practice thus even sharp rabbis; actually in Gemara and poskim not mentioned must specify reason, etc.; but per Rashi and Rashba and Beit Yosef who hold thus — difficult; seems because most vows before sage are fasting or not eat meat and drink wine and similar self-affliction — reason presumably known vowed to justify soul — therefore not customary to ask reason; unlike other matters e.g. not benefit friend — need know reason (end Perishah). What he wrote actually in Gemara and poskim, etc. — incorrect; what Gemara and poskim say must specify vow means specify reason as proven in chapter HaSholeach (35:2) and Bekhorot (end 45) (end) in Gemara and poskim also to justify still question — we do not practice specifying reason in any vow; further per his words why specify reason lest intent not justify soul and we think intent was justify soul — also Gemara and poskim imply must specify in every vow; rather what we say must specify vow is vow-maker's obligation not that sage must ask; one not specifying reason vowed unspecified without reason; one who vowed for reason and does not explain — sin in his hand — do not bring proof from fools.`,
    '15#א': `And some forbid, etc. Bach end this siman wondered how possible not released after the fact — if so difficult for Rav Nachman chapter HaSholeach Gittin (35:2) need not specify vow — why not fear forbidden matter, etc. (end); wonder at his wonder — per his words why challenge Rav Nachman — halachah not like him — challenge Rav Papa himself who said must specify vow due to forbidden matter; if you say after the fact not released forbidden matter — why fear forbidden matter — would never go to sage for release since never permitted at all; further Gemara challenges per view need not specify from Mishnah vowed and transgressed — lest sage permits — what difficulty — never permitted at all; rather certainly proven entire sugya permitted after the fact; but not relevant to our case — there entire sugya vow not mitzvah or forbidden matter but dependent on forbidden matter e.g. konam fruits if I transgressed or will transgress certain sin and like konam sustenance if I benefited from my ketubah as stated explicitly there — therefore in such case we do not permit forbidden matter but permit vow of sustenance he forbade unlike here vow is forbidden matter and we permit vow to gamble or do forbidden act. Such case no proof permitted after the fact. Thus responsum Rabbi Eliezer Mizrachi siman 50 — gambling vow and such have no release even after the fact.`,
    '15#ג': `They release him, etc. — Rav seems follows his view wrote responsum siman 103 in my view none dispute Rabbi Tuvia on this, etc.; responsum Rabbi Eliezer Mizrachi there wrote in forbidden matter even rabbinic only e.g. gambling and such do not release even if he cries cannot stand my oath — we pay no attention (end); likewise Beit Yosef below beginning page 472 responsum Rashba and Ramban siman 252 dispute this; likewise end Sefer HaKolbo in name of Rashba responsum; Perishah and Bach this siman in name of Maharam Mintz all this specifically gambling with no other occupation and disqualified testimony for that (Choshen Mishpat siman 34) — but person whose occupation not that and sometimes plays — can release.`,
    '18#_': `And I think good, etc. — lest vow takes effect after Passover and if Shavuot festival in city he transgresses vow — therefore release once after Passover i.e. before Shavuot even eve of Shavuot and once after Shavuot.`,
    '20#ד': `For any favor, etc. Mordekhai chapter HaSholeach only when that one compelled and vowed per his intent whether from love or fear, etc.; majority poskim hold favor specifically required; thus Taz's view. Beit Yosef wrote Rashbatz worker can retract within his time even if accepted cherem and excommunication to work all that time can ask on acceptance not with employer's will — one forbidden benefit from friend release only before him per Rabbeinu Tam meaning specifically swore due to favor done with him therefore — his words incorrect — even per Rabbeinu Tam worker does favor employer gives work to earn livelihood (end). I say his words correct — unspecified worker does favor because will do work — but favor not because will swear for him — we require favor specifically due to oath as Ribash siman 461 and Rav wrote; however responsum Ram Alsheikh siman 57 implies called favor — wrote on one who rented house to friend for time and swore not evict within time — called favor because gives rent money (end) — doubt.`,
    '20#ו': `Like Moses — as written Moses agreed to dwell with the man meaning vowed per Yitro's intent not return to Egypt.`,
    '20#ז': `And oath was for benefit, etc. — I do not know what he means; if to exclude where oath not for friend's benefit — even with favor can release per this view — no — Rosh, Tur and poskim in name of Rabbeinu Tam imply no distinction — always one who received favor cannot release; likewise Ribash there except some say for friend's benefit even without favor cannot release (end); but with favor though oath not for friend's benefit — no view permitting; possibly because some say in latter section only where benefit to friend does shame apply — otherwise no shame — he would say no benefit told me — thus from challenge in HaSholeach and end chapter Mumim (end) — examine. Beit Yosef in name of Ribash: all who friend does not forbid him even if swore for friend's benefit — all agree need not friend's intent — siman 461; Darkei Moshe on this and this is Mordekhai above who required they compelled him — but other poskim do not seem so (end); I think all agree with Ribash — poskim discuss only receiving favor as explicit in their words; Ribash explicitly discusses not receiving favor — examine.`,
    '20#פ': `If died one to whom vowed, etc. — implies even if did favor can release thus; likewise Taz explicitly — doubt from where — responsum Ramban only no suspicion since died — without favor; but if did favor reasonable if died cannot release; thus proved, etc. — difficult — see Chiddushei Rashba chapter HaSholeach and Rosh there and Nedarim Rabbi Eliezer and Semag negative commandments 201 and Ribash siman 370 — from Pharaoh told Joseph release oath to Jacob — all who does not receive favor can release — implies if receives favor cannot release even if died; though can deflect and distinguish and be lenient — needs investigation.`,
    '20#צ': `Some say for mitzvah need, etc. — implies even when swearer received favor — clear; if so why God told Moses release vow before Yitro — he needed then for God's mission to redeem Israel — no greater mitzvah — doubt (end Taz); concealed from him Ribash siman 287 source of this law who brought these Tosafot and asked this very difficulty: seems contradiction to Tosafot — no greater mitzvah than Moses walking to Egypt on God's mission yet needed ask Yitro and not released without his will (end); nevertheless can answer Tosafot — Maharik root 52 and Rav below regarding mitzvah per many — not called mitzvah matter except where mitzvah impossible without releasing oath; but wherever can fulfill without nullifying vow — no; likewise Moses — much salvation for place could redeem Israel without Moses returning to Egypt. Also say God's will was not go until release vow — if not released not mitzvah.`,
    '20#ש': `And see siman 239 one who swore, etc. — should read as there seif 8; Taz also wrote in error.`,
    '21#ב': `And some are stringent, etc. — because their regrets unequal — one regrets for this reason another for that; but for mitzvah matter all agree and regret with one regret — thus stringent view; per this if regret with one regret can release (and one who accepted cherem or niduy per many can release with their intent — all agree cherem need not opening and regret as above seif 8 and siman 215); likewise Ramban and Mechaber seif 30; further I think all agree when many come and regret though regrets unequal can release each per his regret — stringent view only not release unspecified with one regret — thus I think; not like Beit Yosef's implication — see there; however responsum Mayim in end Sefer HaPela seems doubtful in all cases not release per many unless mitzvah matter (end); see responsum Rabbi Eliezer Mizrachi siman 50 also stringent not to release discretionary matter even per many agreeing.`,
    '21#ח': `One who swore not benefit from father, etc. Darkei Moshe wrote hence implies even swore on mitzvah itself they release — not responsum Ramban siman 1000 Ran (end); likewise poskim' implication and Gemara chapter HaSholeach beginning (Gittin 36) incident of teacher Rabbi Acha forbade him (end); some explain swore per friend — some say do not release for mitzvah he swore on without his intent as above s.k. 52 — presumably per many agree to release in every mitzvah matter.`,
    '21#י': `And some say if vowed, etc. Maharik root 52 wrote if those three involved in that vow — all agree per intent of many — presumably those standing before him; Beit Yosef and Darkei Moshe; if said to them per your intent I vow — clear all agree per many and better than saying per so-and-so, etc.`,
    '23#ב': `Has release without their knowledge. Beit Yosef in name of Rashba responsa — need not release before them; proven in several places chapter HaSholeach; implies even without those many knowing they released — only for one who vowed per individual they said thus (end Beit Yosef); likewise proven HaSholeach beginning Gittin 36 and Bekhorot end chapter Mumim — in what Gemara challenges there; but per many who have benefit in that vow — otherwise no — no less than individual forbidden due to suspicion as above s.k. 41.`,
    '24#_': `Husband annuls, etc. — since not dependent on her will — husband annuls even without her regret as her will does not prevent annulment — so per many intent does not prevent; Rosh chapter HaSholeach: possibly even if husband retracts intent and permits her vow per others — nothing since woman unspecified vows per husband's intent — Torah tied annulment to husband — husband can retract permission (likewise Rabbi Yerucham netzach 2 ch. 2 in name of some); I think even though Rosh wrote possibly nevertheless holds law so; therefore wrote plainly in hints; if not difficult for Rav Huna why lost ketubah — give husband permission — why Rosh, Tosafot, Ran needed explain annulment of husband helps even per many — proves Tosafot, Rosh, Ran even if gave permission does not help; proven all poskim ruled like Rav Huna — not Bach siman 234 who wrote Rosh doubtful; also omitted Tur Even HaEzer siman 96 plainly even if husband gave permission annuls (end).`,
    '25#ד': `And some say even if stipulated, etc. — all community act as if stipulated thus from start; possibly also Rosh if said per many or per distant communities — no release; Mechaber's view Rosh also does not dispute first view; what he wrote even if stipulated can retract and release — means via inquiry; therefore Rosh's words siman 229 seif 4.`,
    '30#ג': `However some dispute, etc. — doubt per Rav's understanding — Ribash siman 461 (and 285) wrote likewise Ramban in his cherem judgment and Mechaber's words are Ramban's responsum; therefore seems to distinguish — Ribash only when entire community swore together unspecified — not per many since custom to do so; but Ramban's responsum each swore individually — per many — therefore cannot release until all agree; doubt.`,
    '32#_': `Unless they stipulated, etc. Beit Yosef concludes Ribash wrote on one who transgressed community enactment written one who transgresses cherem and niduy — no doubt law treats as niduy — whoever knows he transgressed; but others who did not practice from now on — enactment per custom they accept transgressor bears sin but community or knowers do not seize his stumbling block until proclaim — as if written explicitly in enactment (end); in another responsum wrote thus in name of Rashba (end) — Ribash and Rashba dispute on this Aharon; seems why Mechaber omitted end of Ran's words.`,
    '35#א': `But niduy, etc. responsum Ram Alsheikh siman 49 if decreed cherem applies even one who changes place and vows in other city — cherem applies to future generations; also possible even if not specified applies since one could say per siman 214 s.k. 7 when no intent to retract do not impose place stringencies when he left — here cherem is personal skull, etc. (end briefly); see above seif 29.`,
    '36#_': `To ascend, etc. responsum Maymoni chapter 6 Laws of Oaths even if vowed per many has release when wife pregnant and danger to take her to Land of Israel — do not say leave wife; likewise Acharonim in name of Maharam Mintz if wife unwilling to go even vowed per many in distress has release for such great mitzvah.`,
    '37#_': `Unless he says, etc. — meaning says his intent when swore conditionally that friend would agree to matter — if so when friend disagrees not vow at all; what he wrote that so-and-so can release means will not agree to matter; likewise Ran chapter Naarah HaMe'orasah end (Nedarim 73): however where stipulated explicitly said I vow if my friend wants — friend can release — need not special language — anywhere says I do not want suffices (end); Beit Yosef brings — since like conditional vow friend need not say permitted or forgiven, etc.; likewise below siman 239 seif 2 Hagahah; but one who vowed absolute vow not tying to friend's agreement but says friend can release like sage — nothing as siman 231 — once vowed takes effect — no power to uproot Torah that only sage or three laypeople release. See what Mechaber wrote friend can release means actual release and helps when nullifies at time of vow while not yet taken effect as siman 231 Hagahah — thus Mechaber's view and correct per law (except slight doubt Beit Yosef page 472 beginning per many on Ribash and Ran and Tur siman 231 stringent, etc.).`,
    '41#_': `One who swore, etc. responsum Maharam Padua siman 72 brought Rashba responsum siman 74 wrote explicitly if said his vow and fixed time at once — one matter; if time passed vow void; but when swore to do matter and fixed completion time — two matters not dependent; see there he expanded.`,
    '45#ב': `Who vowed in time of distress, etc. if vowed to fast and cancels from study — permitted release that vow because Torah study equals all and forbidden to fast causing cancellation; also slaughter, inspect, teach can release — mitzvah if no other available — Bach siman 64 in name of responsum Rosh in collection; seems likewise if vowed per many can release since considered mitzvah matter; however one who fasted does not harm him to cancel — obvious cannot release; likewise Bedek HaBayit in name of Rashba responsum mitzvah all agree ascetic cannot stand on Torah and reason — all per his nature; some eat bread with salt and enjoy, some afflicted not sitting on meat pot — each acts per nature (end); see above s.k. 95.`,
    '45#ד': `However if they released, etc. — per his view above s.k. 21; but already explained there s.k. 16 per many no release even after the fact; if so here too; what Rav wrote here unspecified vow in distress after the fact permitted and above s.k. 21 vow per many wrote do not rely except in constraint and need — because Darkei Moshe wrote on responsum Beit Zahav and abbreviated responsum Ramban siman 223 vow in distress has release therefore after the fact permitted (end) — meaning what written above responsum Ramban siman 254 sick vowed not eat cheese harmful to illness now regrets — no release with regret because do not open with regret unless original regret — certainly when vowed needed forbid cheese harmful — now desires and wants release — therefore no release except other openings (end); can question cheese reason was harmful — otherwise would release via regret; even vow on cheese wrote release via opening (meaning anyway they released via opening — vow in distress obviously not released even via openings) — implies release for vow in distress since vowed in illness; however general sugya not release unless mitzvah matter as below this siman (end Darkei Moshe); in my view astonishing what connection responsum Ramban siman 223 to vow in distress — vow in distress like responsum Beit Zahav siman 266 like Jacob vowed if God be with me and guard me on this way, etc. this stone, etc. and all You give I tithe; responsum Maharam Padua siman 72 one vowed in distress if leave prison go to Land; Maharam Mintz and Acharonim one vowed in distress go to Land or fast; Bach in name of collection Maharik one in great danger at sea vowed if saved go to Jerusalem, etc.; unlike sick vowed not eat cheese because harmful — reason in Beit Zahav responsum vow in distress no release because one who vows per friend who did favor cannot release without intent (and above seif 20) a fortiori before God who answered in distress — no greater favor — who says God agrees to that release — not relevant to cheese vow in illness — therefore seems cheese case can release even ab initio but vow in distress not even after the fact — thus I think (however responsum Ram Mintz wrote vow in distress has release proved from Agudah chapter 4 Nedarim and other poskim he expanded).`,
    '46#א': `But one who swore, etc. Perishah this siman examine his language — begins with vows ends with oaths — seems because dispute between Rashba and Rosh in vows whether take effect on each other (and siman 215) — why end with oath not vows — all agree oaths do not take effect; see siman 229 seif 3 if forbade loaf twice and released — forbidden until ask second time — implies not released with one release (end) — no proof from siman 229 — there only asked on first; if comes ask on all at once suffices.`,
    '46#ב': `Need release for each one, etc. Maharal ben Chaviv responsum siman 139 ruled even vow and oath on one matter need not release for each — if wants can release all at once; if releases first alone second and third take effect and need release; if releases third alone first and second take effect — proved from Rambam chapter 6 Laws of Oaths (end); likewise Bach and Ran responsum siman 17 (Mechaber below siman 229 seif 5) discusses specifically when said second vow only after asked on first and third after second — while not asked on first second vow not in world and third; but one who swore or vowed twice thrice e.g. will not eat today will not eat today — all oaths in world — cannot say here do not release until takes effect — one release suffices (end) — seems main; nevertheless stringent ab initio since possible. Maharal ben Chaviv himself saw one who practiced release each separately; responsum Ma'amar siman 48 also stringent.`,
    '48#_': `One who swore, etc. — likewise every Rosh Chodesh whose beginning is two days — unlike swore pay on first of Nisan which is full day — not obligate pay beginning of day — different here days divided and we grasp day of Rosh Chodesh by itself; therefore seems proper to be concerned and stringent — biblical doubt stringently; thus Rashba wrote and Semak cited Choshen Mishpat siman 73 s.k. 27 in unspecified; in my humble view without doubt to be stringent we rule thus — as Gemara chapter Konam even for leniency say thus because called first of month; explained above siman 220 seif 4; possibly Rashba's view to distinguish with difficulty — I do not think to distinguish; likewise Rosh chapter Konam as written there therefore in documents on first Rosh Chodesh day write on Rosh Chodesh, etc. — document law like vow law in people's language; per this even without oath if document obligates pay Rosh Chodesh Adar or before first of Adar — since document language judged like vows as Rabbi Yerucham and Shulchan Aruch Choshen Mishpat siman 42 and Beit Yosef Choshen Mishpat siman 73 from 15th in name of Rashba responsum — if so obligated pay before first of month — thus seems to me.`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '5#_': `Forbidden to lend on his vow to sage, etc. Yerushalmi chapter HaShutafim thus; Tur brings and concludes forbade benefit of townspeople — asks sage of town. Tanna taught even initially asks sage of town — not like annulling his own vows; implies Tanna who forbids — reason like annulling his own vows; likewise Beit Yosef in name of Ribash siman 406; Beit Yosef challenged — annulling own vows different — written do not profane his word; no difficulty — not saying truly annuls own vows but like annulling with kaf of comparison — since city sage benefits from this release — after release permitted to benefit — like conflict of interest fear will not examine well — already learned must be settled in matter — doubt thus; truly like monetary judgments — any judge with conflict cannot judge — here likewise; Rabbi Akiva permitted for son of Kalba Savua vow in chapter Afi Al Pi — Ribash answered he did not release himself but via others joining; nevertheless after the fact permitted — not true vow as law as we wrote nearby. What Yerushalmi and Tur conclude forbade townspeople's benefit asks sage — Beit Yosef challenged — he benefits from city sage who releases vow — therefore explains opposite as clear in his words — but forced — unnecessary; per his view siman 231 seif 2 one forbidden to learn from forbidding person laws and aggadot — why not forbid — via knowing laws permitted what thought forbidden — simple this not called benefit teaching prohibition and permission — here likewise; Beit Yosef brought below Rashba responsum swore not be collector, etc. as next seif forbids — seems disputes Yerushalmi and Tur.`,
    '10#ב': `That person is not brazen, etc. — since reason lest not in heart — this opening does not help if others open thus — ashamed answer truth in heart; but if one comes himself saying I ask release my vows because now clear sin in vowed vow or when not releasing vow like building in what was opening good and helps — not relevant he brazen to say would not refrain — could be entirely silent; Talmud and poskim precisely said do not open in honor of God — implies we do not open but he himself can open; likewise case do not open except in existing matter, etc. seif 12 — likewise as Maimonides wrote chapter 6 Laws of Oath do not open nolad, etc. concluded but if he regrets himself due to nolad and changed mind — they release (end); likewise Rema seif 13 but if regrets from outset, etc. — one topic with do not open in honor of God — thus seems to me.`,
    '11#_': `Such as if forbade them from property, etc. Gemara reason since was brazen before him to vow — also at regret if no complete regret will be brazen; law's reason like reason seif 10 i.e. any common transgression we do not say ashamed to say though knew prohibition would vow; say since truly regrets likewise matter removing sustenance obligation though uncommon — since even without Torah command reason dictates honor father as nations do and considered transgression by all — he was brazen breached fence — certainly no shame now to say had I known obligated in sustenance would not vow — certainly not from shame since no shame left only true regret. Maharshal attached distinction in these laws do not stand and do not visit — likewise honoring father explicit in Torah — since already brazen transgressed Torah will be brazen now and not ashamed unlike opening making transgression in vow not revealed to all; his words astonishing — if already knew prohibition do not stand or honor father what opening applies to release vow from these had you known, etc.`,
    '12#ב': `And this so-and-so becomes Torah scholar. Tosafot chapter Afi Al Pi challenged — Rabbi Akiva released for son of Kalba Savua vow saying per great man's intent who vowed — not open nolad; answered since Rabbi Akiva went to study hall and customary going to study hall to learn becomes great man necessarily. Answer forced — sugya seems son of Kalba Savua did not know at vow Rabbi Akiva went to study hall; from Semag and responsum Ramban Beit Yosef brings another answer — they explain do not open nolad means do not open but open with regret due to nolad; Rema seif 13 brought therefore no question from Rabbi Akiva — there was complete original regret for son of Kalba Savua due to nolad becoming Rabbi Akiva scholar; explicitly said regrets from outset due to this — did not forbid nolad except if others open so he regrets due to nolad and he says nothing regrets from outset only says per this intent did not vow — we say regrets only from nolad onward not from outset; same if after others' statement he explicitly regrets himself from outset helps; clear from Rambam Beit Yosef brings here wrote but if he regretted himself, etc.; though Beit Yosef from Rabbi Yosef Bekhor Shor above does not imply — nevertheless Rema ruled well as great ones; in my view Bekhor Shor also agrees since explicitly uproots vow from outset and regrets himself.`,
    '14#_': `Must specify vow and reason to them. Reason in Gemara Rashi widow woman lest vowed for orphans forbid all fruits if I benefit from ketubah — if later comes unspecified to release vow forbidding fruits without completing if I benefit from ketubah — stumbling block; if completes entire vow sage will not release; from this learn everyone who vowed must complete all vow words before sage lest if completes all sage will not release; also said reason in Gemara forbidden matter — lest vowed forbidden all fruits if transgresses sin he is accustomed to — if does not complete at release whether transgresses sin only forbids fruits alone — stumbling block; Yerushalmi brought incident before Rabbi Yoden asked release vow — what vowed — not comfortable — do people act thus — to gamble I mean — said blessed chose sages' words must specify vow — if not specified would release (end). Beit Yosef seems must specify vow vowed not also why vowed, etc.; Rashba responsum must specify reason for which vowed — main matter like chapter HaSholeach and not comfortable case (end) — clear we must beware two things: first not omit from vow if conditional like first case; second even without condition not substitute vow language — say as is like not comfortable who intended substitute gambling vow with clean language so sage not understand — seems Shulchan Aruch intent here specify vow and reason i.e. specify vow not substitute language like not comfortable; specify reason means not shorten what said at vow if I do certain sin; but if says at vow exactly as vowed without substitution or shortening — no hint in Gemara and poskim sage asks what reason in heart why made this vow; on contrary proven Yerushalmi opposite — if not strange to Rabbi Yoden would not ask why vowed thus — from difficulty must investigate — unlike other vow no immediate difficulty — thereby novelty — require specify vow must say language as is even without shortening; this included in must specify vow. Found explicitly like my words in Tosafot chapter She'eir HaYetomim (Arakhin 23) Mishnah one who consecrates property and wife has ketubah — Rabbi Eliezer when divorces forbids benefit lest artifice divorce collect ketubah from consecration and return — Rabbi Yehoshua no need; Gemara Rabbi Eliezer's reason vow in public no annulment per Rabbi Eliezer — Rabbi Yehoshua holds has annulment therefore vow ineffective; Tosafot on Rabbi Yehoshua you asked even has annulment must specify vow — if comes before sage will not release; answer need not specify except language vowed not must explain why vowed — unlike Gittin case priest married woman in sin — vow divorce her transgress then divorce — said must specify — why sage not release — there vows not benefit disqualified wife when before sage specifies vow sage will not release; here will not prevent release even hears language vowed (end) — explicit as I wrote proven Gemara explicitly like Tosafot — thus seems correct; Perishah wrote here wondered never saw practice even sharp rabbis; Gemara and poskim never mentioned specify reason; can distinguish only required specify reason in strange vow like not comfortable but normal vow no forbidden concern — however Rashi and Rashba wrote generally their proofs show in all cases; therefore do not deviate since Beit Yosef and Rema wrote generally like them; seems because most vows before sage fasting or meat/wine self-affliction — reason presumably justify soul — not customary ask; unlike other e.g. not benefit friend — need know reason (end); per what I wrote never intended except beware shortening condition i.e. reason and language substitution; if said vow as is need not ask what in heart at that hour in any vow — no stumbling block even if reason unknown since vow not tied explicitly to anything — thus clear to me.`,
    '16#א': `And does not make agent. Beit Yosef in name of Rashba wrote even per view who holds thus nevertheless via writing permitted — handwriting superior to agent; Ribash conclusion Beit Yosef brings rules like Rambam — never release except before him — Jephthah's daughter proves (end) — implies even via writing forbidden — if not Jephthah could also send writing.`,
    '18#_': `And seems good to me, etc. Reason clear in Beit Yosef — since prohibition at that hour Shavuot festival enters — do not wait until Shavuot since some hold vow dependent on days — after Passover arrived and not paid prohibition applies on Shavuot though Shavuot not yet arrived since certainly will come as above Ran — therefore good release after Passover not specifically immediately after Passover but even day before Shavuot only beware not transgress at Shavuot entry; since also some hold e.g. Rambam vow not take effect on days until prohibition day — must release second time after Shavuot day entered. Levush wrote reason must release after Passover lest immediately after Passover obligated leave city so not in city on Shavuot — not precise at all in source — no prohibition until Shavuot; need not release immediately after Passover whenever wants only before Shavuot; seems better release close to Shavuot eve and on Shavuot itself — Beit Yosef brings Ran responsum swore not be in city on Shabbat — doubt if vow takes effect near Shabbat and release then or not until part of Shabbat entered then release; depends interpretation scripture but on first day rest, etc. i.e. when first day entered part; written seven days leaven not found — verses contradict — therefore say this first day refers eve of Passover — likewise here forbidden at Shabbat entry and some say first day meaning not contradictory — Ran concludes release near Shabbat darkening and per Shulchan Aruch here need release twice — likewise here Shavuot case release first near Shavuot and second on Shavuot itself — thus correct.`,
    '22#ב': `If swore for discretionary matter. God did not command thus swear — do not say language per God except threat on them; some explain oath of accepting Torah God commanded swear thus.`,
    '37#_': `And see siman 231. Meaning if that one per whose knowledge nullified before vow took effect voided — Beit Yosef here does not imply from Ran's words brought — proven from several places chapter HaSholeach even without those many knowing released; only per individual vow they said thus (end); proven HaSholeach and Bekhorot end Mumim — in Gemara's challenge; but per many with benefit in vow — otherwise no — no less than individual forbidden suspicion as s.k. 41. What Mechaber wrote he can release means actual release helps when nullifies at vow time while not taken effect as siman 231 Hagahah — see Ran chapter Naarah end: however where stipulated I vow if friend wants — friend can release — need not special language — I do not want suffices; Ribash one who says per so-and-so does not imply can nullify even at hearing unless stipulated explicitly; nevertheless swore per friend now says intent that so-and-so could release — we listen (end); Rav did not intend here except refer siman 231 if explicitly says that one can nullify his vow.`,
    '41#_': `Since was obligated, etc. I do not understand Rema here — responsum Ramban siman 259: swore pay by set time or invite daughter to wedding day so-and-so and did not — can argue obligated afterward every day transgresses oath — two matters: swore do something without tying time; accepted do by set time — not dependent; but swore do matter on day so-and-so if passed no oath obligation — lashes for transgressing oath exempt from doing; brings proof from fast forgot ate — unspecified day lost fast needs another day; if this day does not lose fast only completes that day (end). Rema here distinguished first and second section — astonishing where hinted this distinction — both sections refer same matter in responsum; certainly cannot distinguish except Maharam Padua or Maggid Mishneh distinguish with lamed versus with bet; Rema added not obligated further but obligated aside from oath — latter phrase superfluous if not from oath; more astonishing responsum to Ramban is Rashba responsum as Beit Yosef introduction — explicitly this law responsum Rashba siman 84: swore do matter fixed completion time — though not done by that time still obligated because two matters: swore do and accepted by time — not dependent; swore do on day so-and-so if passed no oath (end). Here Shulchan Aruch from Ramban responsum first section two distinct matters swore essence and time as wrote explicitly swore and fixed time; therefore first and second same condition; Maharam Padua siman 72 brought Rashba responsum wrote explicitly if said vow and fixed time at once — one matter; if time passed void; if swore do and fixed completion — two matters; Rosh general 8:6 even if not demanded within time oath stands — time spur essence on payment; swore eat loaf today — not obligated eat that loaf essence on time; here obligated pay essence payment time spur; Maharam Padua Rosh does not dispute since document language; Ribash once time passed exempt; Rashba; though Rosh opposite not plausible; even Rosh can be lenient here debt payment differs; another responsum distinguish as Hagahah here whereas not his case; Rav distinguished first second — words astonishing — cannot distinguish except Maharam Padua or Maggid Mishneh lamed versus bet; wrote some say — Rosh's view; Taz challenged; more astonishing per Hagahah Rashba i.e. first view here implies per disputant Rosh even without prior obligation oath applies afterward — not so — like swore eat loaf today as Rosh explicitly responsum; for law rule like Rosh stringent — obligation without oath oath applies after time; without obligation distinguish say to day versus on day; Maharam Padua there vow distress ascend Land Iyar war afterward no further oath penalty but stringent release; practice stringent.`,
    '43#_': `And likewise if exchanged religion, etc. Rosh general 34 law 1 — Beit Yosef did not bring here; we testify engagement not made intending family disgraced; nevertheless heard if occurred with sister of father's father — no such explicit law found only what explicit disgrace was; if not except they stipulated, etc. Beit Yosef concludes Ribash on one transgressing community enactment written transgressor cherem niduy — no doubt law niduy treatment; others not practicing from now enactment per custom transgressor bears sin but community not seize stumbling block until proclaim — as written in enactment; another responsum Rashba (end) — Ribash and Rashba dispute Aharon; why Mechaber omitted Ran's end.`,
    '48#_': `On first day of month. Since in vows follow people's language and in people's language first also called Rosh Chodesh though on Rosh Chodesh itself not obligate pay beginning of day — different here two days divided — biblical doubt stringently; Beit Yosef in name of Ribash; per Rambam above siman 220 seif 8 if knew leap year forbidden until second Adar Rosh Chodesh — here if knew at vow two Rosh Chodesh Adar days — not obligated until second; a fortiori from there dispute Rabbi Meir Rabbi Yehuda which is Adar — halachah Rabbi Meir second Adar called Adar though against people's language calling first Adar after Shevat — a fortiori here all hold second Rosh Chodesh is main Rosh Chodesh for anything we say intended until second Rosh Chodesh — no doubt — thus I think.`,
    '49#_': `Half to this, etc. Beit Yosef in name of Rashba derives from half to Aaron half to sons — were it not holy would say unlike there half to Aaron is law Choshen Mishpat siman 253 seif 24 dying man said property to so-and-so — different there giver intended from start half each unlike here said pay debts — seems all debts; likewise build synagogue — all synagogue building — obvious one swore pay debts means all; not exempt paying part; proof Shulchan Aruch siman 229 seif 1 but if vowed pay friend and friend released part — rest not released — implies language to pay him means entire debt obvious; also language swore pay friend Choshen Mishpat siman 73 insufficient giving partial payment; if said money for debt payment and synagogue building — like case mentioned would mean money aids payment not all payment from this and synagogue building likewise; but saying pay debts means this will be payment not other things; must say error they thought could pay all debts and build synagogue from money — insufficient — vows in error void from start or per that they pay debts and remainder build synagogue; even per Rashba divide — if community says intent was thus we listen as Beit Yosef Ribash this siman: nevertheless clear one swore per friend now says intent that so-and-so could release — we listen as Mishnah unspecified vows stringent explanations lenient — this case like first sections meaning not to be more stringent than lenient; Ribash also wrote end siman hear to be lenient many matters interpret them a fortiori here can interpret their words per our explanation — thus seems Rashba also agrees.`,
  },
};
